1. Introduction
Accessibility is a key principle, acknowledged by the United Nations, to enable individuals living with a disability to live independently and participate fully in all aspects of life [1]. Driving a car, allowing one to be independent in his/her own transport, is a potentially very helpful solution against social disability [2]. However, to resume driving is a complicated process, especially following a traumatic event, or in case of neurological and neurodegenerative diseases. In fact, these conditions might impair cognition, visual perception, and motor function, which can increase the risk of crashes by interrupting driver information processing at sev-
