Driving simulation is largely used both in industry and in academia. In industry, it is used for dynamic evaluation, chassis and powertrain systems development, estimate of fuel consumption, road design, human machine interaction, and autonomous driving [11]. Simulators can be divided in two groups: (i) lowﬁdelity desktop simulators speciﬁcally designed for education; (ii) simulators having a more complex architecture, which combine one of more of the following components: vehicle dynamics model, visual, auditory, and haptic cues. Some of them also include motion cueing and kinematics algorithms and motion system to further increase the realism of the simulation [11].
Overall, high-ﬁdelity driving simulators cost runs in the one hundred thousand dollar range, require large amounts of space and are hence not useful in a clinical environment, or for home training [12]. Also, most of them, are focused on physically replicating a real car [13], but the burden of transferring from wheelchair to the simulators´ seat is a highly restrictive barrier for people with motor disabilities [9].
Another important but not yet completely fulﬁlled need, is the possibility to create and personalize scenarios with various diﬃculty elements [14,15]. Even if high-ﬁdelity simulators provide the possibility of creating and personalizing driving scenarios, these systems are usually designed to fulﬁll many different purposes at the same time (i.e., driver impairment, autonomous vehicles, energy system) [11], and are not optimized for the evaluation and training of people with disability [16]. Furthermore, an analysis of the driving simulators speciﬁcally designed for rehabilitation of driving skills, highlight that such systems are simple tools composed of a software application (either screen based or with HMDs) and driving controllers, that do not provide the possibility to create personalized scenarios [16] .
The goal of this project is to develop ADRIS, an open-source, highly accessible, realistic virtual reality (VR) driving simulator, with programmable scenario speciﬁcally designed for people with disabilities. The system replicates the natural interaction between the user and physical world (steering wheels, button controllers), also providing patients the possibility to virtually drive in a variety of environments allowing them to practice driving freely during their hospital stay or at home. The system offers the possibility to use a VR head mounted display (HMD), also referred as VR headset, to increase the sense of being immersed in the environment and the subject’s engagement. Moreover, a semi-immersive version (e.g., curved screen) of the system has been developed to manage situations where the use of HMD is not possible (e.g., individual problems of the user with 3D immersive VR) or is not required. The user can be presented with many of the situations that may occur while driving, such as various daylight or weather conditions, pedestrians, or other vehicles unexpected moves. The simulation platform provides a variety of measures associated with driving and compliance with traﬃc rules, thus allowing the evaluation of the subject’s behavioral responses (e.g., reaction time). The system integrates the acquisition of bio signals to investigate the re-learning process and the compensatory/adaptive mechanisms that occurs during driving practice at the level of the muscle activation.
Other than the HW/SW design and implementation steps, here it is presented the result of a preliminary test performed on a cohort of healthy impaired subjects aimed at determining: (i) users’ acceptance, realism and immersivity levels; (ii) workload of using a VR-based simulator; (iii) occurrence of motion sickness or any other discomfort; (iv) whether ADRIS can provide a realistic driving experience; (v) if the simulator guarantees the extraction of reliable behavioral parameters. This test was performed to verify if the system could be proposed to people suffering from sensorymotor disability, both for assessing and training of their driving

skills, with the long-term goal to give them the prospect of returning to drive.
2. Related work
One of the main research areas is driving skills evaluation and training under different circumstances (e.g., alcohol or drugs consumption, occurrence of fatigue and sleepiness) [11]. As examples, Stough et al. [17], used using placebo and real drugs to assess that both MDMA and methamphetamine negatively affect driving performance using a driving simulator. Similarly, simulant and opiate drugs increase the occurrence of risky behaviors, resulting in a compromised driving ability [18]. Another study that investigates emotional and stress factor during driving, reported that older drivers underestimate stress levels, with respect to younger drivers [19].
Driving rehabilitation and evaluation of driving skills in driver with sensorimotor disability have gained more importance in the last decades, as it has the potential to improve road safety and it is commonly recommended to those who, because of illness, accident, or injury, would like to drive again [20]. Pavlou et al. [21] discovered that drivers with spinal cord injury had a conservative driving behavior caused by self-awareness of their degraded driving performance. This attitude was characterized by a low average speed, and low variability of the average speed. However, another study reported that ﬁve sessions of simulator training can improve driving ability [22]. In general, studies comparing driving performance of people with disability with those of healthy controls agree that the former have a poorer performance [23,24].
Typically, driving abilities are investigated by collecting performance data, subjective feedback and, more rarely, behavioral measures such as eye or head movements [11,25–27]. To the best of our knowledge, a few studies using driving simulators have also collected physiological data, mainly for detecting fatigue [28–31]. Schmidt and Bullinger [28] have used eye tracking, heart rate and skin conductance measurements; other groups instead measured cortical activity [29–31]. While fatigue has received considerable attention during the years, emotional state is still an open ﬁeld. Paschalidis et al. [32] collected heart rate, electrodermal activity, blood pressure and temperature to measure emotional states. Similarly, Rendon-Velez et al. [33] assessed the effect of time pressure by recording eye and head movements, as well as electrocardiogram, thorax expansion and limb movements.
3. Methods
3.1. Hardware
The system integrates two types of commercially available controllers: a 4-axis Joystick (Logitech WingMan Attack 2) and a Pedals Steering Wheel PlayStation (Logitech G920 Driving Force Wheel; Fig. 1).
Moreover, ADRIS includes two visualization devices: a 32-inch curved display (SAMSUNG LC32F391FWNXZA), and an immersive HMD (HTC Vive; HTC Taiwan) combined with a Leap Motion Controller (UltraLeap, USA), to track the user’s hands in real time, replicating them in the VR environment (Fig. 1). The simulator can be used also with additional controllers ranging from keyboard and mouse, up to adaptive driving aids, as well as with different HMDs (e.g., HTC Vive or Oculus).
Finally, the simulator comprises a synchronization system allowing the simulator to be coupled with systems for the acquisition of biological data, such as brain (i.e., electroencephalography) and muscle activity (surface electromyography). During a preliminary study, real time electromyographic signals have been col-

