3.2. Software
ADRIS is based on the graphic engine Unreal Engine 4 (Epic Games Inc., North Carolina, US) and CARLA (Car Learning to Act [34]), an open-source simulator for autonomous driving research for urban driving developed by Intel Las, Toyota Research Institute and Computer Vision Center of Barcellona [34] CARLA is based on a client-server architecture, with the server being responsible for the entire simulation. With this architecture, it is possible to create different clients (i.e., cars), and to manage various scenarios or weather conditions. Also, CARLA provides several features that have been adapted to ﬁt the purpose of having a ﬁrst-person simulator. Among them, there is Traﬃc manager which is the module controlling vehicles inside the simulation. Traﬃc manager has the goal to populate the simulation world with multiple vehicles that can either run with autopilot or can be used to develop a speciﬁc task in a scenario (i.e., a vehicle suddenly stopping, or running the red light) [34]. Other useful modules are Scenario Runner that provides urban, rural, and other traﬃc scenarios (see below) and OpenSCENARIO describing synchronized maneuvers that involve multiple vehicles, pedestrians, traﬃc lights and traﬃc rules [34].
Starting from Unreal Engine and CARLA, ADRIS has been developed by adding the following features (Fig. 2): (i) a ﬁrst-person player module that enables to use the car as a ﬁrst-person driver; (ii) a VR module which makes the simulator usable with HMD; (iii) a car module managing the main components of the car (i.e., controllers and car sounds). (iv) an input/output interface to control the car and send synchronization signals to the biological data acquisition system.
The main vehicle is characterized by its own movement physics, including motor brakes, steering angle, and activation curves. Also, it is possible to change the seat’s position and to perform a 20° side view, as well as a 10° rotation for the monitor version, that, respectively replicate the left/right movements and the back rotation of the driver’s head. In addition, a Heads-up Display showing velocity, gear, and x, y axes for the joystick drive is included in the vehicle.
Regarding the VR module, ADRIS has implemented the FirstPerson camera for VR mode. The blueprint of the new camera ﬁrst enables the HMD, then it tracks the eye level and sets the relative

location of the camera. The VR conﬁguration includes also Leap Motion Controller to track the user’s hands in real time.
After having implemented the main components of the simulator, new blueprints have been added to manage the following props: (i) driving controllers replicating the movements of real controllers into the virtual world; (ii) realistic sounds varying according to whether the car moves or stops (namely, engine sound, handbrakes, gears change, horn, and environmental noises); (iii) input/output interface; (iii) data savings. The input/output interface is the core of the ﬂexibility of the simulator, allowing to use different types of driving controllers. As soon as all the drivers are installed on the graphic engine, it is possible to associate keys and axis, thus making the controller usable with the simulator. Data savings includes x-y-z location of the vehicle (sampling frequency 30 Hz); biological data (EMG activity of 14 muscles; sampling frequency 2 kHz); driving performance, namely the distance driven, duration of the simulation, number of collisions and infractions (i.e., running red lights; running stop signs; lane crossings).
3.3. Driving scenarios
As mentioned above, the driving scenarios can be created by using the Scenario Runner tool. All the information about a new scenario (name of the session, road type, weather, light conditions, etc.) is contained in an XML ﬁle, which is checked by Scenario Manager, prior to starting the simulation. In addition, Scenario Runner has been edited by adding a new log system. As soon as the simulation starts, the log system sends a trigger signal to synchronize the simulation with external acquisition systems (e.g., the EMG); then, at each time frame, the timestamp and location of the vehicle are saved in a ﬁle. In addition, information about the driving performance and the distractors are stored for subsequent analyses, and each simulation is recorded via CARLA for replays.
The combination of Unreal Engine 4 and CARLA, allows to create maps, vehicles, and pedestrians, to assign routes and autopilot to vehicles. Moreover, CARLA offers a set of Python nodes to generate new simulation scenarios, change the weather settings and lights conditions and spawn a selected number of cars in the scene. In addition, customized road geometries can also be added by using external editors such as Road Runner (Matworks), that is an interactive editor to design 3D scenes to simulate and test autonomous driving systems. With this software, it is possible to customize a road plan by adding 3D elements, create new maps, signals, and other signage. Also, Road Runner imports lidar point

