To test the system, nine custom designed scenarios (Table 1) organized in three levels (i.e., three scenarios for each level) with various diﬃculty in terms of route complexity, visibility, and trafﬁc condition (Table 1; Fig. 3) were used. Each scenario has a main event happening at the beginning, in the middle or at the end of the simulation, and minor events along the entire simulation. Main events include a car passing through a red traﬃc light, a cyclist crossing the road or an object blocking the street (Table 1); minors are traﬃc at a junction, other cars making unexpected maneuvers, red lights, junctions to cross, stop signs or traﬃc to be managed. The ﬁrst level is based on a map characterized by 4lane roads, a residential 2-lane road, various traﬃc lights with a big junction and a tunnel. It also has a roundabout in the center of the map (Fig. 3; Table 1). The second level takes place in a medium traﬃc urban setting with 2-lanes roads and an 8-lane expressway (Fig. 3; Table 1). The third level is characterized by a rural scenario without horizontal signs and by narrow roads (Fig, 3; Table 1).
3.4. Subjects
For this study, 17 people (average age: 26.06 SD: 4.02 years; 10 females) participated in one experimental session. Inclusion criteria were to have a driving license and no known history of orthopedical injuries or neurological disorders. None of the participants played videogames daily, and 7 of them played videogames

a couple of hours a month. 13 out of 17 subjects drove less than 4 h a week and three participants drove more than 1 h a day. Finally, 3 subjects wore glasses, and 4 participants suffered from car sickness in real life. The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the Department of Informatics, Bioengineering, Robotics and Systems Engineering (DIBRIS), University of Genoa, Genoa, Italy (code CE DIBRIS protocol - 009/2020 approved on 18/05/2020) and conformed to the ethical standards of the 1964 Declaration of Helsinki. Each subject provided written informed consent to participate in the study and to publish individual data.
3.5. Experimental design
The experimental sessions used the monitor version of ADRIS (i.e., with 32-inch curved monitor as a visualization device; Fig. 1) and the pedals and steering wheels as controllers. As a ﬁrst step, each participant ﬁlled out a background questionnaire to assess if he/she meets the inclusion criteria and to give consent to take part into the study.
The questionnaire included questions about driving habits, video-game use, vision deﬁcits and corrections, and car sickness in real life. During the experiment, the activity of 14 muscles has been recorded (bilaterally triceps brachii, biceps brachii, brachioradialis, ﬂexor carpi ulnaris, trapezius descendens, latissimus dorsi and right-side tibialis anterior and soleus; Fig. 4). The EMG electrodes were positioned according to the guidelines of SENIAM (Surface EMG for Non-Invasive Assessment of Muscles, seniam.org), which is a European project on surface EMG.

