After the experiment, subjects ﬁlled out three questionnaires: (i) Simulator Sickness Questionnaire [35] (SSQ) to assess motion sickness during the use of the simulator; (ii) NASA Task Load Index [36] (NASA TLX) to investigate subjective workload perception; (iii) Igroup Presence Questionnaire [37] (IPQ) to determine the users’ sense of presence inside the VR.
3.6. Data analysis
Starting from trajectory data of the vehicle provided by the simulator (x,y,z; sampling frequency 30 Hz), the average and maximum speed has been computed off-line by applying a Savitzky–Golay ﬁlter and its standard deviation. For each scenario, the simulator collected the number of collisions (either with other vehicles or with objects and buildings) and the number of infractions (i.e., wrong lane, running red light, running stop sign). As the data were not normally distributed (Kolmogorov-Smirnov tested), Mann U-Whitney tests between the ﬁrst and the last test’s indices has been performed to investigate practice-related changes.
EMG data were recorded at 2 kHz; raw signals were ﬁrst bandpass-ﬁltered between 40 and 450 Hz by using a ninth-order band pass Butterworth ﬁlter, then rectiﬁed and low-passed ﬁltered (fourth-order Butterworth ﬁlter, cut-off frequency 4-Hz) to obtain the envelope. Finally, each signal was normalized by its maximum value to make it comparable across different muscles and subjects [38,39]. All analyses were performed using custom developed software written in Matlab (Mathworks Massachusetts, USA; version 2020b).

4. Results
4.1. Questionnaires
Results from the SSQ questionnaire report no general discomfort (general discomfort median 1, range: [1–2]; Fig. 6a). No signs of motion sickness symptoms have been detected (nausea: 1, [1–3]; stomach awareness: 1, [1 – 2]; vertigo: 1, [1 – 3]; sweating: 1, [1–3]; increased salivation: 1, [1–3]; burping: 1, [1–2]; diﬃculty concentrating: 1, [1–4]; diﬃculty focusing: 2, [1–4]; headache: 1, [1–4]; dizziness: 1, [1–3]; Fig. 6a). As expected, three out 17 subjects reported a moderate level of eyestrain (median: 2; [1–7]) and blurred vision (1; [1–6]), although most of the participants did not experience any vision related discomfort.
The NASA TLX survey reported no signs of physical fatigue (1; [1–3]; Fig. 6b) and a moderate level of mental effort (mental demand: 4; [1–6]; overall effort 3; [1–7]; Fig. 6b). Two out of 17 subjects reported a sensible level of frustration in performing the experiment (2; [1–6]). Score on subjective performance ranged from 1 to 8 (6; Fig. 6b).
The last questionnaire was the IPQ to test the subjective sense of being in a virtual environment (Fig. 6c). The ﬁrst question asked was about the realism of the world surrounding the user: nine out of 17 reported that the virtual world felt irreal (score <5; median and range of all the responses: 4 [1–7]). Similarly, half of the participants did not ﬁnd the simulation coherent with the real experience (score <5 n = 8; median and range of all the responses: 5 [2–8]). This is probably because the experiments were run in a lab with the monitor version that might have affected the overall realism of the simulation. Interestingly, 11 subjects felt surrounded by the virtual world (score >5; median and range of all the responses: 6 [4–8]) and were fascinated and engaged by the simulation (median and range of all the responses: 7 [3–9]). Also, 14 drivers felt present in the virtual world (score >5; median and range of all the responses: 6 [4–8]) and rated positively its quality (median and range of all the responses: 7 [3–9]).

