4.2. Performance
On average, participants drove 11.5 ± 0.4 km (mean ± STD) in less than an hour (55 ± 4 min), due to the large amount of traﬃc lights requiring to stop multiple times. Data of all subjects have been averaged to establish whether practice related effects occurred. Visual inspection of the data shows that max and mean speed (considering only when the car was moving; i.e., speed > 5 Km/h) were the highest in the second training (expressway; Fig. 7a,b), together with the speed variability (Fig. 7c), which indicates whether the driver maintained the same speed during the driving. Finally, analysis on infractions suggests that infractions augment in a low signalized scenario (Fig. 7d). Interestingly, the number of infractions signiﬁcantly decreased with practice (pretest: 1.54 ± 1.45 posttest: 0.69 ± 0.95; p = 0.042; Fig. 7d).
4.3. EMG
The muscle strategy was not expected to be unique for all the subjects for two reasons: (i) driving is subjective in terms of positioning and behaviors (some drivers are calm and relaxed, while others can be rush drivers); (ii) even though simulations occur in the same scenario, participants could have experienced different driving conditions due to their own speed (e.g., red or green light; being the ﬁrst car at a junction or having to wait for the other cars to cross it). Indeed, muscular activity at this stage should be ana-

lyzed on a single subject basis. Fig. 8 shows the muscle activity of a representative subject for the whole duration of a single scenario. As expected, the anterior tibialis was the most used muscle, as it is involved for accelerating. Similarly, the right soleus was responsible for a sudden braking (Fig. 8, top panel). The latissimi dorsi had almost a constant activity for the whole duration without variations. Differently, the arm muscles and the trapezii had activations likely due to curves in the path. In particular, the representative subject seems to mainly use the left arm to accomplish the curves (Fig. 8, bottom panel).
5. Discussion
VR represents an attractive option to consistently study relearning processes by replicating situations that users usually experience while driving in the real world [9]. Experimentally, VR brings ease in creating controlled and safe, yet complex and dynamic environments and extends the capabilities that the realworld limits. In the context of neurorehabilitation, VR can be used to promote cognitive and motor functional recovery [40]. The beneﬁts of VR rehabilitation have been suggested to result from enhanced motor learning and retention [41]. Overall, interactions in VR maximize motor and cognitive abilities simultaneously (e.g., requiring planning, attention, information processing of stimuli in the virtual environment, sensory integration) [42].

