Within this framework, the authors designed and implemented ADRIS, an open-source ﬁrst person view driving simulator to promote the return to driving of people with disability, and to assess their ﬁtness to drive. The system is based on the integration of an adapted open-source simulator for autonomous driving (CARLA) with customized addons.
Other than CARLA, other research groups have been developed open-source automated driving simulators. In fact, driving simulators can be used for many different applications. Among them, there is autonomous driving which is becoming more important in the last years. As an example, Schroeter et al. [43] combined 180-degrees videos of real roads with a virtual car into a tool for designers and developers to rapidly prototype and test novel incar interfaces and experiences. Indeed, this system does not allow interactions between the end user and the driving scenario [43]. Conversely, Riegler et al. [44] developed an immersive simulator for testing different types of human-computer interactions during autonomous driving. As for ADRIS, interactions between the driver and the car are possible by using driving controllers, as well as head, hands and gaze movements [44], even though the user is required to drive only when the autonomous driving system is not capable to do so. Indeed, neither performance nor physiological data are collected during the simulation [44].
The most innovative aspects of ADRIS concern the combination of a high-ﬁdelity virtual world, as the one used in CARLA, with features speciﬁcally designed for the training and testing of people with disability. This includes the possibility to use adapted controllers, other than commercial driving sets. Also, the simulator can be used with either semi-immersive or immersive visualization devices, thus making it usable in many contexts such as home training, rehabilitation, education, and research. Of notice, the driving scenarios are highly customizable in terms of traﬃc volume, road type, visibility, and distractors. Familiar driv-

ing paths, such as home neighbors or roads that people must learn, can be replicated by using interactive editors for 3D scenes (e.g., RoadRunner, Matworks). Within these maps, it is possible to set distractors, specifying the timing and the location of the event.
Finally, this study presents the ﬁrst step toward the development of a new driving simulator that provides behavioral (i.e., trajectory, number of collision and number of infractions) and biological measures (e.g., EMG in the current version). Also, additional measures such as mean and max speed can be computed oﬄine and can be used to assess the performance and the degree of driving skill of the user.
This is particularly interesting because it will allow, in future studies, to investigate different aspects of driving ability and deﬁne indices and metrics to measure: (i) effects of medication in driving behaviors and subjects’ ﬁtness to drive [45]; (ii) re-learning processes and the compensatory/adaptive mechanisms that occurs during driving [46].
Preliminary results on healthy unpaired subjects, whose main purpose was to test the usability of the system, suggest that the system is well tolerated in terms of physical distress, as supported by users’ feedback (Fig. 6). Interestingly, prior to the experiment we collected information about car sickness and 4 out of 17 participants suffer car sickness in “real life”. Luckily, this was not the case during the simulation, as reported by the SSQ questionnaire. This may be related to two aspects: on one hand, motion sickness tends to be lower when the person drives [47]. On the other hand, using a monitor may mitigate simulator sickness. Monitors delivered monoscopic images, as opposed to VR headset presenting stereoscopic images. As the stereoscopic content seems more realistic, it can also produce a greater discrepancy between expected and perceived sensory information, thus leading to VR sickness [48].

