Results on the NASA Task Load Index, used to investigate subjective workload perception show a moderate level of effort, mental and temporal demand, thus suggesting that additional sessions of training can be required to make the user familiar with the technology used (i.e., simulation environment, controllers), as also reported by studies assessing the validity of mental workload measures in a driving simulation environment and comparing it with real driving [13,49]. This consideration is further supported by behavioral data reporting that the simulator is coherent with a real experience, albeit with some differences. In general, the users were slower than they would be in real life. As an example, the maximum speed in the second level is on average lower than 75 km/h (Fig. 7), even though the scenario takes place in an expressway. This can indicate that the participants were not familiar with VR application, given also the fact that they do not play video games often [50]. Another possibility is that one hour of driving is not enough to be familiar with a new car and a new scenario, as it would be in real life. Indeed, the lower number of infractions at the end of the session, proposes the idea that participants need practice to feel comfortable with the simulation.
5.1. Limitations
This study presents the ﬁrst step toward the development of a new driving simulator for training and testing driving skills of people with sensory-motor disability. Despite the different combinations of hardware that can be generated to use the simulator (e.g., monitor version with joystick controller and brain activity data collection, or VR headset, with steering wheel and EMG recording), only the monitor version, with pedals and steering wheel controllers and EMG, has been tested. This choice was made to test the user experience and to collect homogenous data; however, to conﬁrm the preliminary results, all the combinations should be tested in the future. For the same reason, the study should be replicated in a larger sample of users with different age, experience with technology and in a further step with people with different motor and cognitive abilities. Currently, we have tested the system on 17 healthy unimpaired participants aged 22 to 38 years, recruited among the students and staff of the University of Genoa, Italy. Prior to use the tool presented, more research and validation studies are required, also involving people with different ages, as well as people with sensorimotor disabilities.
5.2. Future work
The current version of ADRIS has been tested with custom designed pre-deﬁned scenarios, as the main goal was to assess the usability of the simulator as a training and evaluation tool. However, in the future, the simulator will integrate an interface to both select pre-existing scenarios designed with therapists and clinicians, and to create their own scenes. This way, it would be possible to provide a fully personalized experience (i.e., by letting the user to drive in familiar areas, where he/she will exploit daily activities).
Another important future development will be to investigate the best ﬁeld of view needed to provide a realistic driving experience. The current version of ADRIS shows a ﬁeld of view of 114 that was chosen to see the road and be present into the simulation when a curved monitor is used. However, in the future, it would be interesting to also provide a three-screen conﬁguration, thus, making the simulator more versatile. In particular, the curved-monitor version could be used for daily training or at home, while the three-screen conﬁguration might be installed in rehabilitation or research center to assess driving skills.
Notably, it will be important to collect simultaneously several parameters, associated with driving and compliance with traﬃc

rules. These include speed, acceleration, lane variation, errors, driving performance, braking behavior, that should be coupled and related to attention, reaction time, eye ﬁxation, mental workload, and physiological measures. Indeed, these metrics will allow the evaluation of subject’s behavioral responses from which to assess the performance and the degree of driving skill of the subject with sensorimotor disability, the effect on driving of medications, the subject ﬁtness to drive and the occurrence of cognitive deﬁcits. The integration of different bio-signals (e.g., pupillary response, electrocardiogram, skin conductance) will allow to unmask the timely contribution of anxiety, arousal, and fear to drive. In addition, the simulator should be adapted for being used by people with different motor impairments, who may need customized hand controls, equal to those they will use during real driving.
Lastly, to have a new tool useful and effective for training and evaluating driving skills, a validation study will be ﬁnalized. The validation of driving simulators is an open research topic, as no standardized method has been provided so far [13]. Systems validation includes: ﬁdelity assessment of the software and the visualization device; reliability, namely the ability of a simulator to report consistent results over time; validity, that is the capability to accurately represent a real-world driving experience [13]. Validity can be absolute if the behavioral parameters collected during the simulation match those of real driving, or relative if the simulators results show the same patterns as real driving (i.e., it can be deﬁned some correlations between simulated and real driving behaviors [13]). Within this framework, it will be important to carefully design the validation study in terms of experimental design, measures to analyze and participant factors, eventually proposing a validation approach usable with other simulators.
6. Conclusions
ADRIS is a prototype of a driving simulator combining highﬁdelity driving scenarios, with customizable features such as adaptable driving controllers, a VR headset, and the possibility to collect behavioral and physiological data. The simulator has been designed and developed to train and test the driving skills of populations with different needs: people with sensorimotor disability, new or elderly drivers; individuals with attentional deﬁcits; people taking medications, with the long-term goal to assess driving ability in an objective and standardized way and provide an adaptive customizable tool for training.
